<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <!--
      #2453 M4: macOS Debug entitlements — deliberately WITHOUT
      com.apple.security.application-groups.

      The iOS entitlements file (ManifoldDemo.entitlements) declares
      group.com.manifoldkit.demo. It has two consumers, not one:
        1. Share/Action Extension -> host app handoff (see
           Example/Advanced/Extensions/). Both extensions are iOS-only
           (SUPPORTED_PLATFORMS = "iphoneos iphonesimulator" on their
           targets), so on macOS nothing ever writes their App-Group keys —
           ManifoldDemoApp.swift's checkForPendingSharePayload reads back
           nil for a structural reason (no extension runs here), not
           because of this entitlement.
        2. AskManifoldDemoIntent (Intents/AskManifoldDemoIntent.swift), an
           App Intent compiled into this app target (not an extension) with
           a real #elseif canImport(AppKit) branch — it DOES run on macOS
           (Shortcuts/Spotlight), writes to the App-Group UserDefaults, then
           opens manifolddemo://ingest for ManifoldDemoApp.swift's
           handleOpenURL to read back. This is a live macOS consumer.

      Declaring the entitlement here would require a Mac App Development
      provisioning profile bound to a registered Mac, which is exactly the
      CI/local-dev blocker this file exists to avoid — see
      Example/README.md "Running on macOS — unregistered Macs".

      Why dropping it doesn't break consumer #2: this app has never enabled
      App Sandbox (com.apple.security.app-sandbox appears nowhere in either
      entitlements file). com.apple.security.application-groups only governs
      cross-container access *under* App Sandbox; unsandboxed,
      `UserDefaults(suiteName:)` is an ordinary shared preferences domain
      regardless of entitlement, and `init?(suiteName:)` only returns nil
      when the suite name equals the caller's bundle identifier or the
      global domain — never for a missing entitlement. So the App-Intent
      round trip is expected to keep working as-is, not because a nil guard
      catches its absence. If App Sandbox is ever enabled on this macOS
      Debug config, this reasoning stops holding — restore the App Group
      entitlement here first, or the round trip breaks for real (a genuinely
      nil `defaults.data(forKey:)`, not the extension-absence case above).
    -->
</dict>
</plist>
